<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ikeda Mitsunaka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1630–<a href="/years/1693">1693</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ikeda Mitsunaka was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> who became lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> domain in 1632, when as a small child he exchanged his domain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-mitsumasa">Ikeda Mitsumasa</a>. His line ruled Tottori, covering the provinces of Inaba and Hoki, until the abolition of the domains in 1871, while Mitsumasa's line held Okayama.</p></div>
